A||N||6||bilbo||Battery won't stay charged....help!!!||||1||
Z||000000||bilbo||06-22-2001||01:15 PM||||Is there a way to find out if it's the battery or the alternator?? Can't you get the car started and then disconnect the battery and if your car stays running it's your battery not the alt.??? I'd rather buy a bat. than an alt!!  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Toddster||06-22-2001||01:32 PM||||Yes. you can disconnect the battery and the car should stay running. If not, then take out the multimeter and check the alternator.<P>If that is fine then you just have a dead battery.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||01:34 PM||||Does the battery go dead when the car is off, or when its running?<P> if when its running then something is wrong with your altinator. For a simple check get a voltmeter (radio shack has em for $12) and check your battery voltage before you start the car. It will be 12.8 V or so if the battery is not dead.<P>  then after you start the car the battery should read 13.8 to 15 V or so - it should be higher than it was with the car off. If its not higher, or if its lower, then the altinator is not working.<P>  If the battery is going dead overnight, with the car off, then either the battery is worn out (every 4 years) or something is not shutting off, like the trunk lite, the heater blower, rear window defogger...<P>  If something is draining the battery this way you need to locate it and fix its switch - a little more complex.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||01:37 PM||||I dont want to counter the Toddsters reply  - but for some cars thats a bad idea. On some cars if you disconnect the battery while the car is running, a good altinator can put out 18 volts or more - likely to blow out your ECM or ignition contol module.<P>  on cars without electronic fuel injection or ignition that is a good test - like on an aircooled VW bug for example.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||06-22-2001||02:33 PM||||Either disconnect the - side of the battery and let the car sit a couple of days, or if you have access to an ammeter shut off the ignition, remove the key, and measure for any current draw.  <P>4 years is usually a good figure to start being suspicious of a battery, although I've had one fail after 12 months (A Champion, hehe) and another after 2 1/2 years; in both cases the charging system on the car checked out OK.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||06-22-2001||02:34 PM||||YA what ken said. You could get a voltage spike and blow the ecm. Go to any auto service if you dont have a gauge/meter. it connects to battery terminals like jumper cables and checks both battery and alternator output, only takes a second.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||06-22-2001||05:19 PM||||YA what ken said. And lots of parts places like AutoZone will test the alternator for you.<BR>. . .and if you have a volt meter and your battery measures less than 12.3 after 5 minutes of rest you gotta be suspicious. Anything in the 11's means you have a dead cell in the battery.||||reg||1
